{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And YHWH spoke to Moses after the death of the two sons of Aaron, etc. And YHWH said to Moses, etc.</b> And the question is: if so, where did the first utterance go? For no command and no communication of matters is mentioned in it. And what Rashi explained — a parable of a sick person, etc. — does not settle, for in the first verse no command at all is mentioned. Now we may say that these four words, 'בְּקָרְבָתָם לִפְנֵי ה׳ וַיָּמֻתוּ' ('when they drew near before YHWH and they died'), are not the narrator's statement to us, but rather the Holy One, blessed be He, spoke these four words to Moses, and this is the text of the first utterance. For HaShem wished to inform Moses on account of which sin the two sons of Aaron died, and therefore He spoke in it a harsh utterance, for it is the attribute of Justice. And afterward He said what follows from this communication, to warn Aaron not to be burned by the coals; therefore 'וַיֹּאמֶר' is stated — a soft utterance." "And the explanation of the matter is that the sons of Aaron were worthy of being struck because of 'וַיֹּאכְלוּ וַיִּשְׁתּוּ וַיֶּחֱזוּ אֶת הָאֱלֹהִים' ('and they ate and they drank and they beheld Elohim') — that they gazed upon the Shekhinah from within a coarse heart of food and drink. And the Holy One, blessed be He, waited for them so as not to intermix with the rejoicing of the Torah. And corresponding to this, this command came to Aaron: that he not enter the Sanctuary on any day in which there is eating and drinking, but only on Yom Kippur, a day of afflicting one's soul, for then he comes in submission before his Creator. Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, set this preface before Moses, and informed him first of the cause of the death of the sons of Aaron in the first utterance, in order to say to him in the second utterance the command 'וְאַל יָבֹא בְכָל עֵת אֶל הַקֹּדֶשׁ' ('and he shall not come at all times into the Sanctuary'). And this is the text of the first utterance that HaShem spoke to Moses after the death of the two sons of Aaron. And what is the utterance? 'בְּקָרְבָתָם לִפְנֵי ה׳ וַיָּמֻתוּ' — He informed him that they died because they drew near to HaShem not at the proper time, as it is written: 'וַיֶּחֱזוּ אֶת הָאֱלֹהִים.' And if you would say, it should have said 'בְּקָרְבָתָם לְפָנַי' or 'אֵלַי' — we find a parallel, as Rashi explained in parashat Vayeira (Genesis 19:24) on the verse 'וַה׳ הִמְטִיר עַל סְדֹם וְגוֹ׳ מֵאֵת ה׳' and so forth. And as for what is written 'וַיָּמֻתוּ,' it should have said 'מֵתוּ' — we find a parallel: 'וַאֲשֶׁר לֹא שָׂם לִבּוֹ אֶל דְּבַר ה׳ וַיַּעֲזֹב' and so forth, where 'עָזַב' is what should have been said. And with this the superfluity of the language 'וַיָּמֻתוּ' is resolved, for behold 'אַחֲרֵי מוֹת' has already been stated — because the first is the narrator's statement to us, and the second is the utterance of HaShem to Moses." "It is also possible to explain 'בְּקָרְבָתָם לִפְנֵי ה׳' — because they were especially close to HaShem, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, was exacting with them to judge them even for a small error, as it is written (Psalms 50:3): 'וּסְבִיבָיו נִשְׂעֲרָה מְאֹד,' for whoever is closer and closer to the king, all the more is he required to be careful with the king's honor. And so Moses said: 'הוּא אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ בִּקְרוֹבַי אֶקָּדֵשׁ' (Leviticus 10:3). And if we may say that there is no earlier and later in the Torah, and this passage was said to Moses before he said to Aaron 'הוּא אֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ בִּקְרוֹבַי אֶקָּדֵשׁ,' then it is plausible to hear that he said this concerning what the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him — the text of these four words: 'בְּקָרְבָתָם לִפְנֵי ה׳ וַיָּמֻתוּ.' And what Rashi explained, that this exhorted him more than the first one, etc. — perhaps his intent is that it was already stated (Exodus 19:22): 'וְגַם הַכֹּהֲנִים הַנִּגָּשִׁים אֶל ה׳ אַל יֶהְרְסוּ לַעֲלוֹת אֶל ה׳,' and there too He exhorted them concerning this, only that there no punishment was stated but rather 'פֶּן יִפְרֹץ בָּהֶם'; therefore it was necessary to exhort him a second time and to say to him that he not die in the manner that his sons died. But if the entire verse, from 'אַחֲרֵי מוֹת' until 'וַיָּמֻתוּ,' is the narrator's statement to us, I do not know from where Moses learned to exhort him not to die in the manner that his sons died — for the Holy One, blessed be He, did not mention to Moses any matter at all about the death of the sons of Aaron. But according to our words it is settled, for Moses learned this from the fact that HaShem communicated to him a reason for the death of the sons of Aaron and joined to it the command to Aaron — surely this is in order to exhort him more than the first time."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {2 "In comment [2], the citation ״תהלים נ״ג״ in the Hebrew likely refers to Psalms 50:3 (whose opening words match the quoted phrase), not Psalms 53; the normalized address is given accordingly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:36:56.090524281Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}